{"causes": ["The power vacuum and civil unrest following Emperor Nero's suicide in 68 CE created an opportunity for new contenders to the imperial throne.", "Galba's ascension to the emperorship necessitated the appointment of loyal and capable military commanders to secure his rule in Rome.", "The Praetorian Guard's growing political power and their expectation of donatives heavily influenced the stability of any reigning emperor."], "effects": ["Cornelius Laco's controversial advice and actions, particularly regarding the Praetorian Guard's donative, contributed significantly to Emperor Galba's unpopularity and ultimate assassination.", "His execution in 69 CE removed a key obstacle for Otho's consolidation of power and further exemplified the brutal nature of imperial succession struggles.", "Laco's tenure highlighted the critical and often dangerous role of the Praetorian Prefect in Roman politics, influencing subsequent emperors' strategies for managing the Guard."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "galba", "sourceName": "Galba", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "cornelius-laco", "targetName": "Cornelius Laco", "context": "Emperor Galba appointed Cornelius Laco as his Praetorian Prefect and relied on him as a close advisor during his brief reign in 68-69 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "cornelius-laco", "sourceName": "Cornelius Laco", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "year-of-the-four-emperors", "targetName": "Year of the Four Emperors", "context": "Cornelius Laco was a key player in the political machinations and downfall of Galba during the tumultuous Year of the Four Emperors in 69 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "praetorian-guard", "sourceName": "Praetorian Guard", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "cornelius-laco", "targetName": "Cornelius Laco", "context": "As Praetorian Prefect, Cornelius Laco's decisions and actions were heavily influenced by the demands and loyalty of the Praetorian Guard, ultimately leading to his demise."}, {"sourceSlug": "cornelius-laco", "sourceName": "Cornelius Laco", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "rome", "targetName": "Rome, Italy", "context": "Cornelius Laco's entire known political career and ultimate fate as Praetorian Prefect unfolded within the city of Rome in 68-69 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "otho", "sourceName": "Otho", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "cornelius-laco", "targetName": "Cornelius Laco", "context": "Otho's successful conspiracy against Galba directly led to the capture and execution of Cornelius Laco in January 69 CE."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Center of activity"}, {"name": "Forum Romanum, Rome", "role": "Site of execution"}, {"name": "Palatine Hill, Rome", "role": "Imperial residence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Cornelius Laco", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "cornelius-laco"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1133873", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornelius_Laco"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Cornelius Laco's brief but impactful role as Praetorian Prefect under Galba directly contributed to the collapse of Galba's regime and the subsequent rise of Otho, profoundly shaping the initial phase of the Year of the Four Emperors.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-20T08:29:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39392625946", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Cornelius Laco was a public servant or civil administrator of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Rome. Known in the fields of military leaders & commanders.", "newValue": "Cornelius Laco, serving as Praetorian Prefect under Emperor Galba, was a pivotal figure in the tumultuous "Year of the Four Emperors" (69 CE), a period that dramatically exposed the fragility of Roman imperial succession. Appointed by Galba upon his ascent to power, Laco quickly became one of the emperor's closest advisors and a powerful administrator, responsible for the security of Rome and the emperor himself. His rise reflects the increasing political influence of military commanders and the Praetorian Guard in shaping the destiny of the Roman Empire after the Julio-Claudian dynasty.

Laco's tenure was marked by political maneuvering and a struggle to maintain order amidst widespread discontent. He wielded considerable power, often advising Galba on matters of state and military affairs, including the controversial decision not to pay the Praetorian Guard the customary donative upon Galba's accession. This decision, along with Laco's perceived arrogance and his role in the execution of Nymphidius Sabinus (another ambitious Praetorian Prefect), alienated both the soldiers and the populace. His inability to secure the loyalty of the Praetorian Guard ultimately contributed to Galba's downfall, as the Guard, discontented and swayed by Otho's promises, revolted.

The immediate consequence of Laco's actions and influence was the swift collapse of Galba's regime. When the Praetorian Guard mutinied in January 69 CE, Laco was among those who accompanied Galba to the Forum, where they were ambushed by Otho's loyalists. Laco was captured and executed shortly after Galba's assassination, his death symbolizing the swift and brutal nature of power transitions in the early empire. His brief but impactful career underscores the volatile dynamics between emperors, their military commanders, and the powerful Praetorian Guard, setting a dangerous precedent for future imperial successions."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-20T08:29:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39392625946", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The power vacuum and civil unrest following Emperor Nero's suicide in 68 CE created an opportunity for new contenders to the imperial throne.", "Galba's ascension to the emperorship necessitated the appointment of loyal and capable military commanders to secure his rule in Rome.", "The Praetorian Guard's growing political power and their expectation of donatives heavily influenced the stability of any reigning emperor."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-20T08:29:11Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39392625946", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "69 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "69 CE"}], "newValue": ["Cornelius Laco's controversial advice and actions, particularly regarding the Praetorian Guard's donative, contributed significantly to Emperor Galba